      "FailureList": {
        "description": "The workspace's most recently failed jobs, newest first.\n\n``jobs`` is a **named collection field rather than a bare list**, deliberately:\n0c2 adds sibling collections here \u2014 its own deliveries, its own unfinished\nwork \u2014 beside ``jobs``, additively, and every client generated against\ntoday's document keeps reading ``jobs`` unchanged. A top-level\n``list[FailedJob]`` would make that same addition a breaking change to the\nresponse's own type instead of a new field a reader may ignore.",
        "properties": {
          "jobs": {
            "items": {
              "$ref": "#/components/schemas/FailedJob"
            },
            "title": "Jobs",
            "type": "array"
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "jobs"
        ],
        "title": "FailureList",
        "type": "object"
      },
      "FailureListInput": {
        "description": "How many of the most recent failures to return.\n\n``extra = \"ignore\"`` mirrors ``core_ops.py``'s ``_IGNORE_EXTRA``: a payload\nthat also names another workspace has those keys dropped and the operation\nproceeds against the context's own workspace.\n\n**``limit`` is bounded, not a bare ``int``.** A bare ``int`` lets ``-1`` reach\nthe ``SELECT``, where Postgres raises *LIMIT must not be negative*;\n``dispatch`` catches that as a handler exception and refuses ``handler_failed``\ncarrying only the exception's class name, when it is an input defect that\nbelongs in the ``input_invalid`` refusal pydantic already produces for a\nconstraint violation. ``500`` is a choice rather than a transcription: the use\nis one screen of failures, and nothing prunes this table in release one, so an\nunbounded ``limit`` is an unbounded read that grows for the life of the\ndeployment.",
        "properties": {
          "limit": {
            "default": 50,
            "maximum": 500,
            "minimum": 1,
            "title": "Limit",
            "type": "integer"
          }
        },
        "title": "FailureListInput",
        "type": "object"
      },
